---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="A Shepherd's Year" description="A seasonal guide to managing a Portland sheep flock through the year.">
  <div class="bg-sage-800 text-white py-20 px-4">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <p class="text-sage-300 text-sm uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">Husbandry</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-5xl mb-6">A Shepherd's Year</h1>
      <p class="text-cream-200 text-xl max-w-2xl">The recurring tasks that keep a Portland flock healthy through breeding, lambing, grazing and winter.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-16">
    <div class="bg-cream-100 border border-cream-200 rounded-2xl p-5 mb-10 text-sm text-gray-600">
      Every flock and farm is different. Adjust timing for your lambing plan, local weather, grazing and veterinary advice. Portlands can breed outside the conventional autumn season, so the calendar should follow the dates on which your ram runs with the ewes.
    </div>
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif prose-headings:text-sage-800">
      <h2>Before tupping</h2>
      <p>Review flock records and planned matings, check kinship, assess the condition and soundness of both ewes and rams, and deal with feet or health issues in good time. Move thin ewes to better grazing where appropriate and make sure the ram is fit for work.</p>

      <h2>Tupping and pregnancy</h2>
      <p>Use a raddle or marking harness to record services and identify repeat matings. Once the ram is removed, calculate likely lambing dates and plan grazing, housing and feed. Pregnancy scanning can help match feeding to litter size and identify barren ewes.</p>

      <h2>Approaching lambing</h2>
      <p>Prepare clean pens and essential supplies, confirm the flock's vaccination schedule with your vet, and watch body condition closely. Portlands commonly produce a single lamb, although twins occur. Check ewes frequently as lambing approaches without causing unnecessary disturbance.</p>

      <h2>Lambing and early growth</h2>
      <p>Ensure each lamb is breathing, warm and receiving sufficient colostrum promptly. Treat navels, confirm the ewe has milk, and keep accurate birth and parentage records. Watch young lambs and their dams particularly closely during cold or wet weather.</p>

      <h2>Spring and summer</h2>
      <p>Plan turnout and grazing rotations, monitor parasites using a responsible worm-control programme, and keep vaccination and identification records up to date. Arrange shearing before hot weather and protect susceptible animals from flystrike.</p>

      <h2>Weaning and autumn review</h2>
      <p>Wean according to lamb growth, pasture and ewe condition. Review replacements and animals for sale, update registrations, and reassess grazing before the next breeding cycle. The quiet period is also the right time to review what worked and plan the coming year.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
